2. What is an array? Write two advantages and two disadvantages.

Array: An array is a collection of elements of the same data type stored in contiguous memory locations.

Advantages:

Fast access to elements using an index.

Easy to traverse and process data.

Disadvantages:

Usually has a fixed size.

Insertion and deletion can be costly.

3. Why is random access in an array O(1)?

Random access in an array is O(1) because any element can be accessed directly using its index without traversing other elements.

Example: A[5] accesses the element at index 5 directly.

4. What are row-major and column-major order?

Row-major order: Array elements are stored row by row in memory.

Column-major order: Array elements are stored column by column in memory.

5. What is a stack? Explain the LIFO principle.

Stack: A stack is a linear data structure in which insertion and deletion take place at one end called the TOP.

LIFO (Last In, First Out): The element inserted last is removed first.

Example: A stack of plates.

6. What are stack overflow and stack underflow?

Stack overflow: Occurs when an element is pushed into a full stack.

Stack underflow: Occurs when an element is popped from an empty stack.

7. What is a queue? Explain the FIFO principle.

Queue: A queue is a linear data structure in which insertion takes place at the rear and deletion takes place at the front.

FIFO (First In, First Out): The element inserted first is removed first.

Example: People standing in a ticket line.

8. Write the four types of queues.

The four types of queues are:

Linear queue

Circular queue

Priority queue

Double-ended queue (Deque)

9. What is a priority queue?

A priority queue is a data structure in which elements are processed according to their priority rather than simply their insertion order. If priorities are equal, FIFO may be followed.

Example: A hospital emergency system.

10. What is a deque?

A deque (double-ended queue) is a linear data structure in which insertion and deletion can be performed at both the front and the rear ends.

11. What is a call stack?

A call stack is a memory structure used to manage function calls. It stores function information, parameters, and local variables. It follows the LIFO principle.

